A||||10||Supernaut||Somebody pinch me!||||10||
Z||000000||Supernaut||08-05-2000||05:49 PM||||Hey everybody!!! I gotta tell you the story behind my latest Fiero purchase.<P>It all started three weeks ago while I was looking through the classifieds in our local newspaper.  I always check out the "Pontiac" section first, out of habit I guess, because it had been over a year since I had seen a Fiero advertised in there.  But this time there was one, here is what I read:<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>86 Fiero, low miles, like new condition, sunroof, a/c, $3,995 OBO. Call Rob xxx-xxxx<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was more than a little interested, but the $4k price tag put me off as I had recently purchased the '86 GT that I'm rebuilding and a parts car.  I decided to call and then went to look at it anyway.<P>It was a gorgeous silver/black SE 2.8 without a flaw!  Garaged since birth execpt for the outings that had only accumulated 50,300 miles on her.  I was impressed, but decided to make myself forget about it and go back to work on the project car.  Two more weeks had passed when I saw the same ad in the paper, but the asking price had been revised to $3,100 OBO.  I didn't pay too much attention since I still had no extra money to play with at the time.<P>My wife and I went out later that day to have lunch and run some errands in our thoroughly worn and rough-running '89 GMC Jimmy - a good truck, but with 163,000 miles under it's belt and a paint job from the days that GM forgot how to make paint adhere to primer.   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/jimmy.jpg">   I purposefully drove down the street where I had looked over the Fiero two weeks before and there it was...still perfect and still for sale.  We stopped to look again and asked the owner if we could take it for a drive, he agreed and we took it out for a while.  We both fell in love with it and realized we had to have it even though we didn't have a spare nickel.<P>When we got back, I noticed the Fiero owner standing by my old Jimmy having a look at it.  Before we stopped I jokingly told my wife that I was going to offer to trade him straight across for the Fiero.  I did just that, and he asked to drive the Jimmy around the block.  In total disbelief I handed the keys to him and said "sure". He came back in a few minutes, got out and asked "do you have the title with you?".  I think he received about thirty seconds of stunned silence from my wife and I before I answered "no, but I can go get it!"...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The Jimmy needed an inspection sticker, and since I had removed the rusty catalytic converter a couple months ago it wasn't going to get one.  The only thing that the guy wanted was for me to put the cat back on and get it inspected and we had a deal!  I agreed to that and spent the next few days looking for the cat I took off, putting it back on, and finaaly getting an inspection sticker on thursday.  Friday evening (last night) we went over to make the trade and it was done.  Here is what we drove off in:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/Dsc00101.jpg">  <P>I am so happy and almost feel a little guilty over making such a sweet deal...but not too guilty   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have lots more pics of her <A HREF="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank>here</A><P>Supernaut<BR>'86 GT (in progress)<BR>'86 SE (sweet)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Supernaut (edited 08-05-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||Cooter||08-05-2000||06:17 PM||||Great trade! I guess it is true- Good things come to those who wait. . . and wait....||||reg||1
